var UcwBk={ye:UcvJ.TAh,Te:"id",We:"name",resources:[{Re:{id:UcpJ(UcvJ.TAh),name:"Application Owner",restrictToFactSheetTypes:["Application"],subscriptionType:"RESPONSIBLE"},Ue:UcvBk},{Re:{id:"5aaa00ff-2f14-445b-9107-d70c013fb152",name:"Application Owner",restrictToFactSheetTypes:["Application"],subscriptionType:"RESPONSIBLE"},Ue:UcvBk},{Re:{id:"5fee1cb3-bba4-4474-8e5d-fe917d04febd",name:"Security Officer",restrictToFactSheetTypes:["Application"],subscriptionType:null},Ue:UcvBk},{Re:{id:"8bb6bbd2-194c-4cfd-848c-7c3d9fceffa8",name:"Enterprise Architect",
restrictToFactSheetTypes:[],subscriptionType:null},Ue:UcvBk},{Re:{id:"080a2064-1d32-4c02-a58b-0e67de399c12",name:"Business Owner",restrictToFactSheetTypes:["Application"],subscriptionType:null},Ue:UcvBk},{Re:{id:"eb839875-27b7-4108-b27a-74ea27e25d07",name:"Process Owner",restrictToFactSheetTypes:["Application","BusinessContext"],subscriptionType:"RESPONSIBLE"},Ue:UcvBk}],Gg:[]};var UcxBk={ye:UcvJ.R7f,Te:"id",We:"id",resources:[{Re:{roleId:UcpJ(UcvJ.R7f),comment:"Example",name:"Application Owner",description:""},Ue:{}}],Gg:[]};var UcyBk={ye:UcvJ.jk,Te:"id",We:"displayName",resources:[{Re:{id:UcpJ(UcvJ.jk),userName:"example@lucidchart.com",email:"example@lucidchart.com",role:"ACCOUNTUSER",status:"ACTIVE",technicalUser:!1,displayName:"Example User"},Ue:{}}],Gg:[]};var UczBk=new Set([Ucud.EVb]),UcABk=Ucntb.rVb;var UcBBk={zg:Ucxd.c3a,Bl:!0,Woc:!0,description:['## Role\n\nDisplay structured clarifying questions when user requests are ambiguous or lack sufficient detail. Collect specific information needed to proceed with a task. Present questions in a clear, user-friendly form format.\n\n## When to Use:\n- USE TO ask questions about context you could never figure out from other tool calls.\n> **EXAMPLE** - User says "I need help with diagraming my project, but I\'m not sure where to start.  Can you help me?"\n- 1. Context is not possible to gather from other tool calls.  Only the user can provide the context you need.\n- 2. So, Immediately use this tool to ask clarifying questions.\n\n## When NOT to Use:\n- DO NOT USE before using other tools that could provide the information you need.\n> **EXAMPLE** -  User says "I\'m confused, can you walk me through this diagram?"\n> **EXAMPLE** -  User says "What improvements should I make to this workflow?"\n- 1. Use a tool to gather context and determine what the user is referring to.\n- 2. If it is still unclear what the user is talking about, *then* use this tool to ask a clarifying question.\n\n## How It Works\n\n- You provide the **contextMessage** and **questionForm**\n- The tool displays them to the user as an interactive form\n- After the user answers, you receive their responses and can proceed\n- If the user responds with **"Skip clarification questions"**, proceed immediately without re-asking — use your best judgment and placeholders for any missing details\n\n## Question Guidelines\n\n- Keep questions **clear, concise, and focused**\n- **Default to multi-select** for any question with predefined options. Most questions accept more than one answer, and the user should be free to pick multiple.\n  - Use **single-select** only when the options are strictly mutually exclusive by nature: binary either/or, ordering, or strict enumerations where exactly one value can apply.\n  - *Never* create "Both", "All of the above", or "X and Y" as a single option — that is a sign you should be using multi-select instead.\n- Provide helpful option labels that are easy to understand\n- Include a **context message** to explain why you\'re asking these questions\n- Keep the number of questions minimal (**2-3 questions max**)\n- Make sure each question is **necessary** to proceed\n- Do not ask for information you already have from context',
"\n\n## Terminator Tool Notice\n\n**This is a terminator tool.** Calling this tool ends the current orchestration loop and waits for user input. Only call this tool when you are ready to stop iterating and return control to the user."]};var UcCBk={zg:Ucxd.epd,Bl:!1,Woc:!0,description:['## Role\n\nPresents up to 4 candidate templates from the template library for the user to choose from. The user selects one and it is inserted into the document. This tool never imports a template on its own — it always asks the user to pick.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- The user asks for you to use a template\n- The user wants to bootstrap a document from a template (e.g. "start with a template about agile", "import an org chart template").\n- The user is in the middle of choosing a template and is now clarifying what kind they want (e.g. you previously asked "what kind of template?" and they replied with a topic, domain, or activity such as "for sprint planning", "a retrospective one", "something for SWOT"). In this case, call ImportTemplate immediately with their clarification as the search term.\n\n## Priority Over Agent Switching\n\nThis tool **takes priority over `SwitchAgent` routing rules** whenever the user has already expressed intent to use a template. If the user originally asked for a template and is now clarifying which one — even if the clarification mentions a topic that would normally route to another agent (e.g. "sprint board", "retrospective", "SWOT") — you MUST call ImportTemplate and MUST NOT call SwitchAgent. The user\'s stated intent to use a template wins; do not redirect them to board generation, diagram generation, or any other agent.\n\n## When NOT to Use\n\n- The user has asked you to generate a custom diagram from scratch — use GenerateDiagram.\n- The user is asking a question about an existing template already on the canvas.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- The tool shows the user up to 4 matching templates as a picker in the chat.\n- If the user ignores the picker, nothing is imported.\n- If no templates match the request, the tool fails and you should tell the user no templates were found.',
"\n\n## Terminator Tool Notice\n\n**This is a terminator tool.** Calling this tool ends the current orchestration loop and waits for user input. Only call this tool when you are ready to stop iterating and return control to the user."],Fga:i18n.get("ai-tool-loading-importing-template-v2")};var UcDBk={zg:Ucxd.VKa,Bl:!0,Woc:!0,description:["## Role\n\nSend a message to the user — such as answering a question, providing a summary, or asking a clarifying question.\n\n**IMPORTANT:** When used to signal task completion, this **must be the *only* tool call in that round**.\n\n## Message Guidelines\n\n### Personality and Tone\n\n- **Friendly, collaborative, and action-oriented**\n- Focus on **clear, concise** communication\n\n### Clarifying Questions\n\n- If a user request is **vague or lacks sufficient detail**, ask clarifying questions before taking action\n- Do not select tools to run if a request could potentially be fulfilled by **multiple different tools** — ask for clarification first\n\n### Honesty About Limitations\n\n- Be **transparent** about what the assistant and tools can and cannot do, without excessive apologies\n\n### Refusal Policy\n\n- **Politely refuse** unsupported requests without lengthy apologies\n- Suggest **relevant alternatives** if appropriate\n\n## Technical Usage\n\n- Use the current date placeholder as `{{currentDate}}` when referencing today's date\n\n## Exclusion Rules\n\n- Do not include unnecessary **filler, greetings, or redundancy**\n- Remove any **internal metadata** (diagram IDs, item IDs, coordinates, technical identifiers, etc.)\n- *Never* provide **links or URLs** directly, whether in markdown format or not\n- Do not mention **tool internals**, schemas, or post-processing\n\n## Suggested Actions\n\n- You may optionally include **1-3 suggested follow-up actions** as clickable buttons via the *SuggestedActions* parameter",
"\n\n## Terminator Tool Notice\n\n**This is a terminator tool.** Calling this tool ends the current orchestration loop and waits for user input. Only call this tool when you are ready to stop iterating and return control to the user."]};var UcEBk={zg:Ucxd.q3f,Bl:!0,description:'This tool executes a SPARQL SELECT query against a semantic dataset to retrieve matching resources.\nIt is a more powerful alternative to QuerySemanticResources, allowing querying by resource relationships and actual fields in the resource schemas.\nHowever, it requires a deeper understanding of the dataset\'s structure and SPARQL syntax to use effectively.\n\n## When to Use\n- When you want to query resources based on specific fields in the schema OR relationships.\n\n## When NOT to Use\n- You want a specific resource by id or name (use QuerySemanticResources).\n- The current SPARQL engine can only make queries based on resource types, relationships, and named fields in the resources\' schemas. If the field is not explicitly defined in the resource schema, it cannot be queried with SPARQL.\n- You want to query a field and you don\'t have the schemas yet (use GetResourceSchemasForDataset first)\n- You want to query based on relationships and you don\'t have the relation definitions yet (use GetSemanticRelationDefinitions first)\n\n## Fallback\nIf this tool returns no results, fall back to QuerySemanticResources with equivalent filters.\n\n## Query Guidelines\n- The SPARQL query should use urn:lucid:relationship as the relationship namespace prefix.\n- The SPARQL query should use urn:lucid:resource as the resource type namespace prefix.\n- The SPARQL query should use urn:lucid:data as the data namespace prefix for querying based on fields in the resource schemas.\n\n\n## Examples\n- Find all resources of type "Person"\n```SELECT ?node WHERE { ?node a  <urn:lucid:resource:Person> }```\n\n- Find all "Person" resources that have a "knows" relationship to another resource\n```SELECT ?node WHERE { ?node a  <urn:lucid:resource:Person> . ?node <urn:lucid:relationship:knows> ?other }```\n\n- Find all "Person" resources that have the "name" field equal to "Alice"\n```SELECT ?node WHERE { ?node a  <urn:lucid:resource:Person> . ?node <urn:lucid:data:name> "Alice" }```\n\n\n## Warning\n- The SPARQL query should NOT contain any escaped characters, if there are any, the query WILL FAIL.\n- The SPARQL query should NOT contain newlines, if there are any the query WILL FAIL.\n- If the SPARQL query is syntactically malformed the tool will indicate so.\n- Don\'t try the same query multiple times if it fails - revise the query instead.\n- This tool enforces a result cap by default to prevent context bloating. If the cap is hit, the response will include a truncation warning\n  instructing you to call the tool again with `bypassCap` set to `true`.\n\n## Output\nA JSON array of SPARQL binding rows. Each row is an object keyed by the variable names from the SELECT clause, with each value as a plain string.\nNamed-node references to other resources remain identifiable by their URI prefix (e.g. `urn:lucid:node:...`); literals are just their string value.\nProject only the fields you need in the SELECT clause — do NOT always select the whole node unless you need it.'};var UcFBk={zg:Ucxd.fFa,Bl:!0,description:`This tool provides information about available shape libraries

## Search Mode (searchTerm parameter)

Use when you know what shape you're looking for:

- **searchTerm only:** Searches all libraries for matching shapes
- **searchTerm + libraryId:** Searches within a specific library only

Returns matching shapes nested under their libraries and groups. Group matches (where the group name matches but not individual shapes) return the group with an empty shapes array.
Make progressive disclosure calls to get more details about libraries/groups if needed.

## Progressive Disclosure Mode (no searchTerm)

Use when exploring or browsing libraries:

**Level 1 - No parameters:** Returns all libraries with empty groups arrays.

**Level 2 - libraryId only:** Returns that library's groups. If single group, shapes are included automatically.

**Level 3 - libraryId + groupIds:** Returns the specified groups with their shapes.

## Shape Information Returned

Each shape includes:
- **toolboxId**: The class name to use with AddBlock
- **displayName**: Human-readable name

## Custom (User-Created) Libraries

Custom shape libraries appear with a \`libraryId\` starting with \`custom:\` (followed by the library's URI). They are flat — every shape lives in one implicit group, so listing the library returns the shapes directly.

To create a shape from a custom library, pass its identifier as \`customShapeUri\` to CreateBlock (do not use \`library\`/\`variant\`). This is the only way to instantiate freehand-drawn custom shapes.

## Getting Detailed Shape Information
**IMPORTANT**: Before you add any shape to the document for the first time in this conversation, call the **ShapeDetails** tool with its toolboxId. Batch all the toolboxIds you intend to add into a single ShapeDetails call.
ShapeDetails provides:
- **textAreaDescriptors** — the exact \`TextAreaName\` values AddBlock accepts (guessing these causes text to be silently dropped)
- Default background/border colors
- Default dimensions (width, height, aspectRatio)
- Custom property descriptors for advanced configuration

## When to Use Each Mode

**Use search mode when:**
- The user asks for a specific shape (e.g., "add a decision diamond")
- You need to quickly find a shape by name or keyword

**Use progressive disclosure when:**
- Exploring what libraries are available
- Browsing groups within a library
- The user wants to see all shapes in a category

## What data is returned
${Uccpk.FEa}
`};var UcGBk={zg:Ucxd.z3d,Bl:!0,Woc:!0,description:['**Present Plan Tool**\n\nRole:\n- Present the completed plan to the user at the end of a planning session\n- Render the plan in markdown format for easy review\n- Wait for the user to accept the plan or request changes\n\nWhen to use this tool:\n- You have gathered sufficient context and are ready to present a complete, actionable plan\n- The plan covers all the changes needed to fulfill the user\'s request\n- You are in plan mode and ready to hand off execution to the user\n\nWhat a plan is:\n- A list of **tool calls you will issue** once the user accepts — nothing more.\n- Only tools that create, edit, or rearrange content belong as steps. Summarizing, reading, or analyzing the document are never plan steps.\n- **Each step = exactly one tool call.** If two adjacent "steps" would be served by a single tool invocation, collapse them into one step with sub-bullets.\n\nThe single-call test (apply before writing each step):\n- "Could this be accomplished by ONE tool invocation?" → If yes, it is ONE step.\n- Generating a diagram, board, or visual activity is ALWAYS one step regardless of how rich the output is.\n- Applying a formatting rule is ONE step regardless of how many shapes it affects.\n\nHow to write the plan:\n- Start every step with an imperative verb (Generate, Create, Style, Sort, Apply)\n- Write steps and sub-bullets as short fragments, not full sentences\n- For generative steps, add 2-3 sub-bullets capturing the key content details — these are NOT separate steps\n- One step → no numbered list. Multiple steps → numbered list.\n- The plan is a contract, not a conversation\n\nPlan vs. Context:\n- The plan (`PlanContent`) is what you will DO.\n- Caveats, assumptions, and explicit exclusions go in the optional `Context` field — and only when genuinely useful. Do NOT restate things the user already confirmed.\n\nBad plan (treats one diagram-generation call as three steps):\n1. Generate a master workflow diagram\n2. Add a routing decision node\n3. Add converging end nodes\n\nGood plan (single step — no numbered list):\nGenerate a combined master workflow for the two selected flowcharts\n- Decision node routing between "Bug Fixing" and "Steam OSS" branches\n- Shared end node where both converge\n\nGood plan (multiple steps, each a distinct tool call):\n1. Generate a project timeline from the roadmap items\n   - Grouped by quarter with milestones and dependency arrows\n   - Color-coded by team ownership\n2. Apply conditional formatting to highlight overdue items in red\n\nAfter the user accepts:\n- Plan mode will be disabled automatically\n- You will receive a message to execute the plan',
"\n\n## Terminator Tool Notice\n\n**This is a terminator tool.** Calling this tool ends the current orchestration loop and waits for user input. Only call this tool when you are ready to stop iterating and return control to the user."]};var UcHBk={zg:Ucxd.vXe,Bl:!0,Woc:!0,description:["**Suggest Planning Mode Tool**\n\nRole:\n- Proactively recommend planning mode when a user request is complex, multi-step, or exploratory\n- Provide a brief explanation of why planning mode would be beneficial\n- Let the user decide whether to enable planning mode or continue without it\n\nWhen to use this tool:\n- The request involves changes across many items, pages, or areas of the document\n- The request is vague or open-ended and requires exploration to understand scope\n- The request requires gathering document context before a plan can be formed\n- Executing without a plan risks making hard-to-reverse changes\n\nWhen NOT to use this tool:\n- The request is straightforward and can be executed immediately\n- The user has already declined planning mode this session\n- Planning mode is already active",
